    <section id="sect-00004">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00089">A mechanic’s garage purchased 8 brake pads and 5 headlight sets for inventory and paid
        $1,460. Later, the mechanic purchased another order of 6 of the same brake pads and 5 of the
        same headlight sets and paid $1,220. How much does each part cost?</para>
      </note>
    </section>


    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additonal Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Solving a System of Equations by Elimination</title>
        <para id="para-00090">Let’s reinforce the steps to solve a system of equations using elimination.</para>

        <note id="note-00002">
          <para id="para-00091"><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis> - Write both equations in standard form. If any
          coefficients are fractions, clear them.</para>
      <para id="para-00092"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to
          see if the coefficients of one variable are opposites or equivalent.</para>
      <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step
          3</emphasis> - Add or subtract the equations to eliminate one variable.</para>
      <para id="para-00094"><emphasis>Step
          4 </emphasis> - Solve for the remaining variable.</para>
      <para id="para-00095"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis>
          - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the original equations. Then solve for
          the other variable.</para>
      <para id="para-00096"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the solution as an
          ordered pair.</para>
      <para id="para-00097"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Check that the ordered pair is a
          solution to both original equations.</para>
        </note>

        <para id="para-00098"><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00099">Let’s look at an example. Solve the following system of equations using elimination:</para>
        <para id="para-00100">\( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 3x - 3y = -24 \\ -3x + 5y = 30 \end{array}\right. \)</para>
        <para id="para-00101"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write both equations in standard form. If any
          coefficients are fractions, clear them. \( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 3x - 3y = -24 \\ -3x
          + 5y = 30 \end{array}\right. \)</para>
        <para id="para-00102"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to see if the coefficients of one variable are
          opposites or equivalent. The coefficients on the \(x\)-variables are opposites. We can add
          the equations.</para>
        <para id="para-00103"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the equations to eliminate one variable.
          \(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\-3x+5y&amp;=&amp;30\\2y&amp;=&amp;6\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00104"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Solve for the remaining variable.
          \(\begin{array}{rcl}2y&amp;=&amp;6\\y&amp;=&amp;3\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00105"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the
          original equations. Then solve for the other variable.
          \begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x-3(3)&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x-9&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x&amp;=&amp;-15\\x&amp;=&amp;-5\end{array}</para>
        <para id="para-00106"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the solution as an ordered pair. \( (-5, 3) \)</para>
        <para id="para-00107"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Check that the ordered pair is a solution to both
          original equations.</para>

        <para id="para-00108">
          \(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\3(-5)-3(3)&amp;=&amp;-24\\-15-9&amp;=&amp;-24\\-24&amp;=&amp;-24\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00109">
          \(\begin{array}{rcl}-3x+5y&amp;=&amp;30\\-3(-5)+5(3)&amp;=&amp;30\\15+15&amp;=&amp;30\\30&amp;=&amp;30\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>


        <note class="try look-at-me" id="note-00003">
        <title>Try It: Solving a System of Equations by Elimination</title>

        <!--Q#-->
        <exercise id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00020">
            <para id="para-00110">Solve the following system of equations using elimination:</para>
            <para id="para-00111">\( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 4x - 6y = 8 \\ x + 6y = 17 \end{array}\right. \)</para>

            <para id="para-00112">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00020">
            <para id="para-00113">Compare your answer:</para>

            <para id="para-00114">Here is how to solve this system of equations using elimination:</para>

            <para id="para-00115"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write both equations in standard form. If any
              coefficients are fractions, clear them.
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x-6y&amp;=&amp;8\\x+6y&amp;=&amp;17\end{array}\)</para>

            <para id="para-00116"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to see if the coefficients of one variable are
              opposites or equivalent. The coefficients on the \(y\)-variables are opposites. We can
              add the equations.</para>

            <para id="para-00117"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the equations to eliminate one variable.
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\x\;+\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;17\\5x\;&amp;=&amp;\;25\end{array}\)</para>

            <para id="para-00118"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Solve for the remaining variable.
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}5x\;&amp;=&amp;\;25\\x\;&amp;=&amp;\;5\end{array}\)</para>

            <para id="para-00119"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the
              original equations. Then solve for the other variable.
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\\;4(5)\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\20\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\-6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;-12\\y\;&amp;=&amp;\;2\end{array}\)</para>

            <para id="para-00120"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the solution as an ordered pair. \( (5, 2) \)</para>

            <para id="para-00121"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Check that the ordered pair is a solution to both
              original equations.</para>

            <para id="para-00122">
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x-6y&amp;=&amp;8\\4(5)-6(2)&amp;=&amp;8\\20-12&amp;=&amp;8\\8&amp;=&amp;8\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00123">
              \(\begin{array}{rcl}x+6y&amp;=&amp;17\\(5)+6(2)&amp;=&amp;17\\5+12&amp;=&amp;17\\17&amp;=&amp;17\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->

      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
